  <header>
    <h1 class="page-title">How this site is built</h1>
    <p class="standfirst">A site that shows numbers should be held to the same standard as the systems it describes. This page is how it is.</p>
  </header>

  <div class="case-body">
    <section class="case-sec">
      <h2>One source of truth</h2>
      <div class="prose">
        <p>
          The shared public record lives in <code>src/_data/facts.yaml</code>.
          I reconciled it with my résumé on <strong>{{ facts.meta.resume_rev }}</strong>;
          this is the website sync date, not an authored date printed on the PDF.
          <a href="https://www.11ty.dev/">Eleventy 3</a> + Nunjucks templates use those facts,
          while case studies expand on previously documented project details.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="case-sec">
      <h2>A build check that refuses the deploy</h2>
      <div class="prose">
        <p>
          <code>scripts/receipts-lint.mjs</code> runs in CI on every push. It scans the built HTML for four
          kinds of problems: required claims that went missing, superseded figures from older résumé
          revisions that crept back in, internal jargon an outside reader couldn't evaluate, and internal
          links that point at pages the build never emitted. It also checks that the résumé PDF exists.
          The PDF-to-site reconciliation is a separate editorial review; the automated checks cover
          known claims and links. The sources and corrections are listed on
          <a href="/receipts/">sources and corrections</a>.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="case-sec">
      <h2>No internal jargon</h2>
      <div class="prose">
        <p>
          Everything here is described by what it does, never by its internal codename. A reader outside
          Microsoft can't evaluate an acronym, and a write-up that needs a glossary isn't evidence — it's a
          membership test. The translation table lives with the résumé source, and the build check enforces
          it: an internal name in the built HTML fails the deploy.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="case-sec">
      <h2>One résumé</h2>
      <div class="prose">
        <p>
          There is one résumé PDF. Every résumé download points to that document.
          The <a href="/resume/">experience page</a> offers an accessible view with additional
          public project detail. The website and assistant use the shared facts record;
          they are reconciled with the PDF when it changes.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="case-sec">
      <h2>The stack, deliberately small</h2>
      <div class="prose">
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Eleventy 3 + Nunjucks</strong>, built and deployed by GitHub Actions to GitHub Pages.</li>
          <li><strong>Self-hosted type</strong>, latin subsets only: Inter for the interface and headings, <a href="https://fonts.google.com/specimen/Fraunces">Fraunces</a> for editorial accents, IBM Plex Mono for code. No font CDN.</li>
          <li><strong>Plain CSS and JavaScript</strong> — the mobile menu, copy button, run-trace viewer, and portfolio assistant. The résumé, case studies and contact links remain available with JavaScript disabled.</li>
          <li><strong>Analytics:</strong> <a href="https://www.goatcounter.com/">GoatCounter</a> — no cookies, no fingerprinting, counts only.</li>
          <li><strong>Per-page preview images</strong> rendered by a build script from the same templates, committed with the site.</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="case-sec">
      <h2 id="assistant-privacy">The AI portfolio assistant</h2>
      <div class="prose">
        <p>The assistant makes the strongest evidence-backed case for Sarthak and compares public job descriptions with documented experience. It is an AI portfolio advocate, not Sarthak speaking or a firsthand personal reference. The homepage’s guided answers are curated website content, distinct from live AI replies. The typing field stays visible when live replies are unavailable; nothing is transmitted until a visitor sends to a configured service. Its source material is generated from the same approved facts as the portfolio, with links back to the résumé and case studies. Unknown details remain unknown; a role comparison shows evidence and gaps.</p>
        <p>Sending transmits your text and up to two previous questions to Cloudflare and OpenAI. Please use public questions or public job descriptions and omit confidential or personal information. Submitted questions or job descriptions, the assistant’s replies, timestamps, and success or failure status are saved in a private Cloudflare database for Sarthak to review and improve the assistant. The dashboard is restricted to Sarthak’s sign-in. The site does not add raw IP addresses or browser identifiers to these records. Any personal information you include in submitted text will be part of the saved text.</p>
        <p>Records remain in the cloud for at least 31 days and are eligible for automatic removal only after a verified local backup. If a backup is missed, the cloud copy is kept. Local archives remain on Sarthak’s computer until he deletes them. Clearing the chat or reloading only clears the visible conversation in your tab; it does not delete saved records. For a deletion request, <a href="mailto:{{ facts.identity.email }}">contact Sarthak</a> with the approximate date and question so the record can be located.</p>
        <p>Separate temporary rate-limit identifiers and usage counters protect the service. The application requests no saved OpenAI model conversation; Cloudflare and OpenAI may retain operational, recovery, or abuse-prevention data under their own policies. Text entered but not sent and the curated guided answers are not logged as conversations.</p>
        <p>API credentials stay on the server. Input limits, citation checks, rate limits and conservative spending reservations bound each request. If the service is unavailable, the public sources and contact links remain accessible.</p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="case-sec">
      <h2>What gets removed before publishing</h2>
      <div class="prose">
        <p>
          Every write-up is published against a written checklist: customer and account identifiers removed,
          internal system names generalized, absolute volumes stated as orders of magnitude or multiples of
          baseline — while preserving the decision structure, the trade-offs, and the numbers already on the
          public record. Where a write-up has corrected itself, the correction is dated and left visible under
          "What I got wrong".
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

